          "question": "I have both asthma and reflux and my cough is worse at night. Which is more likely to be driving it, and what does guidance describe for each?",
          "answer": "It's important to note that both asthma and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) can contribute to worsening cough, especially at night. Asthma generally involves airway inflammation and can lead to increased coughing, particularly when lying down. On the other hand, reflux can irritate the throat and airways, also causing a cough.\n\nFor asthma, management typically includes avoiding triggers, using prescribed inhalers, and monitoring symptoms. In contrast, managing reflux often involves lifestyle changes such as dietary modifications, weight management, and sometimes medications to reduce stomach acid.\n\nUnfortunately, the corpus does not provide specific guidance on which condition is more likely driving your cough. A discussion with a clinician can help determine the underlying cause of your symptoms and guide effective management.\n\nIf you are experiencing a significant worsening of your symptoms, I recommend seeking immediate care.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
